{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB08806 - MONDO_6896",
  "verbalization": "Roxatidine Acetate is implicated in the pathogenesis of Peptic Esophagitis through a chain of indications that connect it to Gastric Ulcer and Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease, both of which are associated with Peptic Esophagitis. The drug's indication for Gastric Ulcer, in turn, links it to Vonoprazan, a proton pump inhibitor, which is known to treat Peptic Esophagitis. Similarly, Roxatidine Acetate's indication for Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease is associated with Camostat, a drug that is used to treat both Reflux Disease and Peptic Esophagitis. These pathways suggest that Roxatidine Acetate may exert a protective effect against Peptic Esophagitis by targeting the underlying conditions that contribute to its development. The overlapping indications for Camostat and Vonoprazan highlight a potential mechanism by which Roxatidine Acetate could influence the disease process, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated. The evidence supports a plausible link between Roxatidine Acetate and Peptic Esophagitis through its role in modulating gastrointestinal conditions that are closely associated with the disease.",
